1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,392
Two questions.

2
00:00:01,392 --> 00:00:04,888
Pick three of five friends for
a photo, standing in a row.

3
00:00:04,888 --> 00:00:07,292
Pick three of five friends for a picnic.

4
00:00:07,692 --> 00:00:09,426
Same friends, same number.

5
00:00:09,426 --> 00:00:12,499
But one answer is six times
bigger than the other.

6
00:00:12,949 --> 00:00:15,130
Aarav noticed this while
planning the class trip.

7
00:00:15,480 --> 00:00:17,852
How do you know which formula to use?

8
00:00:17,852 --> 00:00:19,212
Arrange, or choose?

9
00:00:19,712 --> 00:00:22,280
There is one question that
decides it every time.

10
00:00:22,280 --> 00:00:23,262
Let us find it.

11
00:00:23,912 --> 00:00:25,838
Here is the deciding question.

12
00:00:26,188 --> 00:00:29,162
Pick any two of the chosen
things, and swap them.

13
00:00:29,162 --> 00:00:30,736
Do you get something new?

14
00:00:31,136 --> 00:00:33,020
If yes, order matters.

15
00:00:33,020 --> 00:00:34,736
It is an arrangement.

16
00:00:34,736 --> 00:00:35,839
Use n P r.

17
00:00:36,239 --> 00:00:38,616
If no, order does not matter.

18
00:00:38,616 --> 00:00:40,145
It is a selection.

19
00:00:40,145 --> 00:00:41,234
Use n C r.

20
00:00:41,884 --> 00:00:44,759
Let us run the swap test on six problems.

21
00:00:45,159 --> 00:00:46,681
A four digit lock code.

22
00:00:46,681 --> 00:00:49,448
Swap two digits, and
the lock will not open.

23
00:00:49,448 --> 00:00:50,406
Something new.

24
00:00:50,406 --> 00:00:51,306
Arrange.

25
00:00:51,441 --> 00:00:53,384
Three toppings on a pizza.

26
00:00:53,384 --> 00:00:56,094
Swap two, and it is the same pizza.

27
00:00:56,094 --> 00:00:56,994
Choose.

28
00:00:57,187 --> 00:00:59,699
Three friends in a row for a photo.

29
00:00:59,699 --> 00:01:02,040
Swap two, and the photo changes.

30
00:01:02,040 --> 00:01:02,940
Arrange.

31
00:01:03,159 --> 00:01:04,059
A handshake.

32
00:01:03,949 --> 00:01:06,459
Ravi shakes Sana's hand,
or Sana shakes Ravi's.

33
00:01:06,459 --> 00:01:07,583
The same handshake.

34
00:01:07,583 --> 00:01:08,483
Choose.

35
00:01:08,521 --> 00:01:10,434
A president and a secretary.

36
00:01:10,434 --> 00:01:12,650
Swap them, and the jobs change.

37
00:01:12,650 --> 00:01:13,550
Arrange.

38
00:01:13,347 --> 00:01:14,857
A committee of three.

39
00:01:14,857 --> 00:01:16,917
Swap two, the same committee.

40
00:01:16,917 --> 00:01:17,817
Choose.

41
00:01:18,215 --> 00:01:19,315
Side by side.

42
00:01:19,315 --> 00:01:22,086
The photo row: three friends from five.

43
00:01:22,086 --> 00:01:23,186
Five P three.

44
00:01:23,186 --> 00:01:24,086
Sixty.

45
00:01:24,208 --> 00:01:26,891
The picnic group: three friends from five.

46
00:01:26,891 --> 00:01:27,901
Five C three.

47
00:01:27,901 --> 00:01:28,801
Ten.

48
00:01:28,978 --> 00:01:29,954
Now you try.

49
00:01:29,954 --> 00:01:31,519
Five people in a room.

50
00:01:31,519 --> 00:01:33,369
Each pair shakes hands once.

51
00:01:33,369 --> 00:01:34,772
Arrange, or choose?

52
00:01:34,772 --> 00:01:35,986
Pause and decide.

53
00:01:39,186 --> 00:01:40,086
Choose!

54
00:01:39,752 --> 00:01:41,647
A handshake has no first person.

55
00:01:41,647 --> 00:01:43,413
Five C two is ten handshakes.

56
00:01:44,063 --> 00:01:46,999
Now, why is sixty exactly six times ten?

57
00:01:47,349 --> 00:01:51,090
To make a photo row, first
choose the three friends.

58
00:01:51,090 --> 00:01:53,534
That is five C three, ten ways.

59
00:01:54,284 --> 00:01:56,625
Then arrange the three you chose.

60
00:01:56,625 --> 00:01:59,815
Three people can stand
in three factorial ways.

61
00:01:59,815 --> 00:02:00,715
Six.

62
00:02:01,093 --> 00:02:03,766
So the photo count is ten times six.

63
00:02:03,766 --> 00:02:04,666
Sixty.

64
00:02:05,161 --> 00:02:09,186
In general, n P r equals
n C r times r factorial.

65
00:02:09,186 --> 00:02:11,405
Choose first, then arrange.

66
00:02:12,155 --> 00:02:14,472
This is the tool for mixed problems.

67
00:02:14,472 --> 00:02:17,361
Choose with C, then
arrange with a factorial.

68
00:02:18,011 --> 00:02:21,877
Watch both counts for a group
of size r, from five friends.

69
00:02:22,277 --> 00:02:24,688
Size one: five groups, five rows.

70
00:02:24,688 --> 00:02:27,148
Size two: ten groups, twenty rows.

71
00:02:27,548 --> 00:02:29,988
Size three: ten groups, sixty rows.

72
00:02:29,988 --> 00:02:33,563
The rows grow faster, because
every group gets lined up.

73
00:02:34,163 --> 00:02:35,063
Quick one.

74
00:02:35,027 --> 00:02:38,967
Is five C three times three
factorial equal to five P three?

75
00:02:38,967 --> 00:02:40,188
Pause and think.

76
00:02:43,388 --> 00:02:44,288
Yes!

77
00:02:43,922 --> 00:02:47,920
Ten times six is sixty, and
five P three is also sixty.

78
00:02:47,920 --> 00:02:49,573
Choose, then arrange.

79
00:02:50,223 --> 00:02:51,190
Remember this.

80
00:02:51,190 --> 00:02:52,090
Swap two.

81
00:02:51,931 --> 00:02:52,897
Something new?

82
00:02:52,897 --> 00:02:53,797
Arrange.

83
00:02:53,539 --> 00:02:54,439
Nothing new?

84
00:02:54,415 --> 00:02:55,315
Choose.

85
00:02:55,711 --> 00:02:57,401
Now, one worked example.

86
00:02:57,751 --> 00:03:02,596
From five boys and four girls, choose
two boys and one girl, and seat them

87
00:03:02,596 --> 00:03:04,454
on three chairs in a row.

88
00:03:04,454 --> 00:03:05,583
How many ways?

89
00:03:06,083 --> 00:03:06,983
Step one.

90
00:03:06,892 --> 00:03:08,104
Choose the boys.

91
00:03:08,104 --> 00:03:10,638
The order of choosing does not matter.

92
00:03:10,638 --> 00:03:12,067
Five C two is ten.

93
00:03:12,817 --> 00:03:13,717
Step two.

94
00:03:13,564 --> 00:03:14,684
Choose the girl.

95
00:03:14,684 --> 00:03:16,050
Four C one is four.

96
00:03:16,800 --> 00:03:17,700
Step three.

97
00:03:17,672 --> 00:03:19,426
Now seat the three people.

98
00:03:19,426 --> 00:03:21,662
Swapping seats gives a new seating.

99
00:03:21,662 --> 00:03:23,217
Three factorial is six.

100
00:03:23,967 --> 00:03:24,867
Step four.

101
00:03:24,863 --> 00:03:25,763
Multiply.

102
00:03:25,647 --> 00:03:27,502
Ten times four times six.

103
00:03:28,252 --> 00:03:30,589
So there are two hundred and forty ways.

104
00:03:31,239 --> 00:03:32,701
Now, the exam trap.

105
00:03:33,051 --> 00:03:35,051
Many students stop after choosing.

106
00:03:35,051 --> 00:03:36,607
Ten times four is forty.

107
00:03:37,007 --> 00:03:41,304
But the question also asks to seat
them, and chairs have an order.

108
00:03:41,304 --> 00:03:45,688
Multiply by three factorial, and
the answer is two hundred and forty.

109
00:03:46,338 --> 00:03:47,616
Where is this asked?

110
00:03:47,616 --> 00:03:51,674
In JEE Main, as word problems that mix
choosing and arranging, like making

111
00:03:51,674 --> 00:03:53,210
words from chosen letters.

112
00:03:53,810 --> 00:03:54,877
Exam level now.

113
00:03:54,877 --> 00:03:55,889
Seven students.

114
00:03:55,889 --> 00:03:59,052
First, second and third
prizes go to three of them.

115
00:03:59,052 --> 00:04:00,823
Meera must win one of them.

116
00:04:00,823 --> 00:04:01,844
How many ways?

117
00:04:01,844 --> 00:04:02,956
Pause and solve.

118
00:04:08,356 --> 00:04:09,833
The answer is C.

119
00:04:09,833 --> 00:04:10,733
Ninety.

120
00:04:11,088 --> 00:04:13,774
The prizes are different,
so order matters.

121
00:04:13,774 --> 00:04:17,050
With no rule, seven P three
is two hundred and ten.

122
00:04:17,550 --> 00:04:20,442
Now remove the ways
where Meera wins nothing.

123
00:04:20,442 --> 00:04:22,898
Then the prizes go to the other six.

124
00:04:22,898 --> 00:04:25,450
Six P three is one hundred and twenty.

125
00:04:25,950 --> 00:04:29,954
Two hundred and ten minus one
hundred and twenty is ninety.

126
00:04:30,554 --> 00:04:32,094
Here is your recap.

127
00:04:32,094 --> 00:04:33,677
Screenshot this card.

128
00:04:36,877 --> 00:04:39,062
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

129
00:04:39,462 --> 00:04:40,362
One.

130
00:04:39,902 --> 00:04:41,326
What is the swap test?

131
00:04:42,226 --> 00:04:43,126
Two.

132
00:04:42,647 --> 00:04:44,596
Eight people each shake hands once.

133
00:04:44,596 --> 00:04:45,777
How many handshakes?

134
00:04:46,677 --> 00:04:47,577
Three.

135
00:04:47,333 --> 00:04:50,913
Why is n P r equal to n
C r times r factorial?

136
00:04:52,013 --> 00:04:55,963
Next time: a triangle of numbers
that expands a plus b to any power.

137
00:04:55,963 --> 00:04:57,279
Meet Pascal's triangle.
